---
.title = "Transports",
.description = "A transport carries CapTP between two peers. The session above it stays the same; what changes is how a peer is named and how the bytes travel.",
.date = .date("2026-06-12T00:00:00"),
.layout = "page.shtml",
.draft = false,
---

A transport, or *netlayer*, is the byte path under a CapTP session. Fetch,
call, promises, and handoffs stay the same no matter which one you pick.
What changes is how you name a peer and how the bytes get there: encrypted
QUIC, a sealed TCP stream, a Tor circuit, a browser DataChannel, or a
cleartext testing socket.

Application code holds capabilities. A netlayer only moves the frames those
capabilities travel in.

# How a peer is named

You reach a peer through an OCapN locator:

```
ocapn://<designator>.<transport>
```

The **designator** is the peer's name for that transport. The keyed and sealed
transports use an Ed25519 public key. iroh uses a node id. Onion uses a Tor
service id. Plain `tcp` uses a `host:port` and is testing-only. Optional hints
can carry routing help, such as the bound address for a key-addressed listener,
but a hint is never permission. [Concepts]($link.page('concepts')) covers
locators and sturdyrefs in full.

# What proves who answered

Every CapTP session signs its hello with a fresh key. That
signature proves the CapTP handshake held together. It does not prove who the
durable peer is.

Who answered is a fact the channel has to supply. On
[Noise]($link.page('transports/noise')), completing the handshake proves the
dialed key. On [iroh]($link.page('transports/iroh')), the QUIC handshake
authenticates the node id. On [WebRTC]($link.page('transports/webrtc')), the
signaling authority binds the peer to the DataChannel. On
[onion]($link.page('transports/onion')), Tor proves the dialed service. A key
written into a cleartext locator is only a dial target until something like
that proves possession of it.

# Picking one

How far you can trust a netlayer is about that byte path, not about whether
CapTP works on it. Cleartext keyed transports still speak CapTP. Anyone who can
see the path can also read and alter every frame.

[Choosing a transport]($link.page('transports/choosing')) is the decision
table: every symbol pancake ships, when to reach for it, how far you can trust
it, and what the path proves. The Noise, iroh, WebRTC, and onion pages cover
setup once you know which path you want. A browser that cannot open a native
socket uses the [relay]($link.page('relay')) as a WebSocket that forwards bytes
to a peer that already has a transport name.
